// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
/*
 * Samsung Galaxy Tab S7 LTE (SM-T875, "gts7l")
 *
 * Forked from upstream's sm8250-samsung-common.dtsi (shared Samsung SM8250
 * phone platform base: PM8150 PMIC, ADSP/CDSP/SLPI scaffolding, base
 * regulators) per the decision in docs/kernel-baseline.md. That base file is
 * phone-shaped (single volume-up key, phone-sized splash framebuffer) and is
 * overridden below wherever this tablet's actual hardware differs.
 *
 * Hardware facts below are sourced from two places, both cited inline:
 *   - Samsung's GPL kernel source for this device (T875XXU1ATK1),
 *     specifically the board-revision overlay that matches our physical
 *     unit's `androidboot.revision=7`:
 *     references/gts7l/arch/arm64/boot/dts/samsung/gts7l/
 *       kona-sec-gts7l-eur-overlay-r07.dts
 *   - Live confirmation pulled directly off the physical tablet (rooted,
 *     stock T875XXU1ATK4) via `cat /proc/interrupts` and
 *     `cat /sys/kernel/debug/gpio`, cross-checked against the above.
 * See docs/hardware-inventory.md and docs/devicetree-notes.md for the full
 * writeup and provenance of every value below. Anything not confirmed by
 * both sources is marked TODO rather than guessed.
 */

/dts-v1/;

#include <dt-bindings/gpio/gpio.h>
#include <dt-bindings/input/input.h>
#include "sm8250-samsung-common.dtsi"

/*
 * Unlike the Galaxy S20 phones sm8250-samsung-common.dtsi was written for,
 * this tablet has a full three-PMIC complex (PM8150 + PM8150L + PM8009),
 * confirmed in docs/hardware-inventory.md from Samsung's own GPL source.
 * common.dtsi only pulls in pm8150.dtsi - add the other two here so the
 * &pm8150l_gpios references below (volume-up, S Pen, MAX77705 irq - all
 * confirmed wired to PM8150L, not PM8150) resolve. Both self-attach to
 * &spmi_bus internally, so #include is all that's needed.
 */
#include "pm8150l.dtsi"
#include "pm8009.dtsi"

/ {
	model = "Samsung Galaxy Tab S7";
	/*
	 * RETRACTED 2026-09-13 (Round 7, docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md): this
	 * 3-entry structure was believed fixed based on indirect evidence that
	 * turned out to be a methodology artifact (same class of mistake as
	 * the "confirmed baseline" retraction elsewhere in this project). A
	 * correctly-built, freshly-tested artifact using exactly this DTS
	 * still hits the identical "No match found for Soc Dtb type" failure,
	 * at a timestamp identical to the microsecond versus every other
	 * variant tried (single-entry, with/without msm-id, with/without
	 * `-@`). The structure below is kept because it correctly mirrors
	 * stock's real appended-DTB layout and isn't known to be wrong - just
	 * not confirmed to be the fix. Root cause of the ABL rejection is
	 * unknown again; don't trust the "fix" framing in the comment below
	 * without re-reading Round 7 first.
	 */
	/*
	 * `qcom,kona` + `qcom,msm-id` fix attempt #2 (2026-09-13), after
	 * attempt #1 (DTS-property-only, single DTB) made zero observable
	 * difference to the "No match found for Soc Dtb type" failure - see
	 * docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md Round 5 for the full comparison.
	 *
	 * Root cause found by extracting the STOCK boot.img's own `dtb`
	 * section (via magiskboot unpack) and inspecting it directly: it is
	 * not one DTB, it's **three plain FDT blobs concatenated
	 * back-to-back** (FDT magic 0xd00dfeed found at byte offsets 0,
	 * 519810, 1039616 - confirmed by hand, not assumed), one per `kona`
	 * silicon stepping this retail unit's kernel expects to possibly see:
	 *
	 *   entry 0: model "kona v2.1 SoC", qcom,msm-id = <0x164 0x20001>
	 *   entry 1: model "kona v2 SoC",   qcom,msm-id = <0x164 0x20000>
	 *   entry 2: model "kona v1 SoC",   qcom,msm-id = <0x164 0x10000>
	 *
	 * All three use plain `compatible = "qcom,kona";` and none carry
	 * `qcom,board-id` at all (board-id belongs to a separate per-board
	 * dtbo overlay, applied later in the boot chain - Samsung's real
	 * overlay for this exact unit is
	 * references/gts7l/arch/arm64/boot/dts/samsung/gts7l/
	 * kona-sec-gts7l-eur-overlay-r07.dts, `qcom,board-id = <0x8 0x7>`).
	 * `qcom,board-id` is deliberately NOT set here for that reason - it
	 * doesn't belong in this stepping-selection blob per stock's own
	 * structure, and attempt #1 including it here proved that placement
	 * doesn't help anyway.
	 *
	 * Attempt #1's single DTB only covered the v1 (0x10000) stepping -
	 * if this physical unit's actual fused silicon revision is v2 or
	 * v2.1 (which stock clearly hedges for by shipping all three), a
	 * lone v1 entry would never match, exactly reproducing the
	 * unmodified failure we saw. The fix built from this DTS (see
	 * docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md Round 5) concatenates three
	 * otherwise-identical builds of this DTB, one per stepping, mirroring
	 * stock's exact structure - see work/build-appended-dtb.sh (or the
	 * Round 5 writeup) for how that's assembled from this single source
	 * file via `fdtput` patching qcom,msm-id per copy.
	 *
	 * Sourcing: stock dtb extraction done locally this session;
	 * cross-checked against Samsung's own base platform file
	 * (references/gts7l/arch/arm64/boot/dts/vendor/qcom/kona.dtsi,
	 * `compatible = "qcom,kona"` + `qcom,msm-id = <356 0x10000>` i.e.
	 * 0x164 0x10000 - matches stock entry 2 exactly) and against the
	 * sibling S9 Ultra project's independently-documented fix for the
	 * identical ABL error string on the same Samsung-ABL family
	 * (references/postmarketos-galaxy-tab-s9-ultra/docs/porting-log.md).
	 *
	 * qcom,board-id ADDED 2026-09-13 (Round 8, docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md):
	 * Qualcomm's own official binding doc (referenced kernel source,
	 * arch/arm64/boot/dts/vendor/bindings/arm/msm/msm-id.txt) states
	 * explicitly that the 2-field short form of qcom,msm-id used here
	 * ("chipset_foundry_id, rev_id") REQUIRES qcom,board-id to also be
	 * defined - a plain documented requirement this DTS was missing
	 * entirely, independent of and much simpler than every DTB-matching
	 * theory chased before it. Stock's own kona.dtsi entries confirm this
	 * requirement (they pair the same 2-field msm-id form with SOME
	 * qcom,board-id value) - fixing "No match found for Soc Dtb type"
	 * (confirmed in Round 9) only needed the property to be *present*,
	 * value `<0x00 0x00>` (wildcard) was sufficient for that specific
	 * check.
	 *
	 * Changed to the real value 2026-09-13 (Round 9, docs/kernel-boot-
	 * debugging.md): after the fix above, a *separate, later* ABL stage
	 * ("Unable to find the Board Dtb" / "Error: Board Dtbo blob not
	 * found") was hit and is NOT satisfied by the wildcard value - proven
	 * across three independent `dtbo` partition content variations (all
	 * produced byte-identical failures), meaning this stage reads board
	 * identity from the *appended DTB itself*, not `dtbo`, and requires
	 * an exact match. Set to `<0x08 0x07>`, this physical unit's real
	 * fused board-id - confirmed via the real per-board overlay in stock's
	 * dtbo.img (`entry.7`, `qcom,board-id = <0x08 0x07>`) and independently
	 * via TWRP's own kernel `Hardware name: Samsung GTS7L PROJECT - PV
	 * REV0.4 (board-id,7)` dmesg line. `0x8` is the platform-type-id field
	 * (constant across all stock's per-region entries), `0x7` the
	 * board-revision field (the value specific to this exact unit's
	 * region/revision). Four independent DTBO content variations (Round 9)
	 * all failed identically even with this value set, tracing further
	 * confirmed board-id alone isn't the remaining blocker.
	 *
	 * qcom,pmic-id ADDED 2026-09-13 (Round 9, via Ghidra decompilation of
	 * the actual ABL matching function, LinuxLoader.pe FUN_00025700):
	 * disassembly revealed ABL treats our appended-DTB match as only
	 * "good enough", not "exact" - an exact match (a specific 6-bit
	 * combination in the match-quality flags word) is required to skip
	 * the strict per-region DTBO search entirely, which is what's
	 * actually failing ("Unable to find the Board Dtb"). One of those
	 * required bits (bit 18) comes from qcom,pmic-id's first cell
	 * matching the live hardware PMIC "model" register exactly - a
	 * property this DTS never had, so that bit could never be set.
	 *
	 * Value: <0x1e 0 0 0> - `0x1e` from the live kernel's own
	 * /sys/devices/soc0/pmic_model (65566 = 0x1001E, low byte = 0x1E),
	 * the closest real evidence available without ABL's own verbose
	 * logging (blocked - needs raw uefivarstore partition writes to set
	 * the standard EDK2 "EFIDebug" variable, not attempted). Per the
	 * matcher's own logic, each of the 4 cells packs one PMIC's
	 * {model: low byte, revision: upper 3 bytes} and this device has 3
	 * real PMICs (PM8150/PM8150L/PM8009) - only cell 0 (the primary
	 * PMIC, matching what the Linux sysfs value itself reports) needs to
	 * be an exact model match for the specific bit we need; a model
	 * value of 0 in the other cells is treated as a harmless wildcard by
	 * the same matcher, not an error. **Not yet hardware-tested** - the
	 * exact correspondence between this Linux-reported value and what
	 * ABL's own hardware read returns is unproven; see
	 * kernel-boot-debugging.md Round 9 for the full reasoning and what
	 * to try next if this specific value doesn't work.
	 */
	compatible = "samsung,gts7l", "qcom,kona", "qcom,sm8250";
	qcom,msm-id = <0x164 0x10000>;
	qcom,board-id = <0x08 0x07>;
	qcom,pmic-id = <0x1e 0x00 0x00 0x00>;
	chassis-type = "tablet";

	/*
	 * RETRACTED 2026-09-19 (Round 22, docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md): the
	 * "regression" described below was real but the context has changed
	 * completely - back then this whole DTB was what ABL itself had to
	 * accept (Rounds 4-11's entire fight), so any content change could
	 * plausibly affect ABL's own matching. Since Round 20/22's uniLoader
	 * pivot, this DTB is never seen by ABL at all - it's embedded
	 * directly in uniLoader's own binary and only reaches the real
	 * mainline kernel after uniLoader has already gotten full CPU control
	 * from a completely unrelated, unmodified stock DTB/DTBO. There is no
	 * remaining mechanism by which content here could affect ABL
	 * acceptance. Re-adding the framebuffer override below on that basis.
	 *
	 * Original note (2026-09-12, Round 5-era): a `chosen`/
	 * `simple-framebuffer` node reusing the ABL's real splash region was
	 * tried to get an on-screen boot console, since neither
	 * `/proc/last_kmsg` (bootloader-only) nor `console-ramoops-0` (only
	 * ever reflects whichever kernel is *currently* running) can show
	 * what our kernel does after handoff. Reverted at the time after an
	 * isolation test showed a regression - not revisited until now,
	 * because at the time it wasn't understood *why*, and the DTB itself
	 * was still the thing ABL had to accept.
	 */
};

/*
 * Round 29 (2026-09-19, docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md): reserve Samsung's
 * real `sec_log_buf` region (`sec_log=0x200000@0x9F200000`, confirmed
 * live via this device's own `/proc/cmdline`) so the kernel's own memory
 * allocator can never hand this physical range out to anything else -
 * `init/main.c`'s `early_sec_log_init()` writes this project's own early
 * printk output directly into it, byte-compatible with the format
 * TWRP's own `/proc/last_kmsg` already reads. `no-map` isn't set
 * deliberately (matching stock's own splash-region convention elsewhere
 * in this file) - `early_sec_log_write()` accesses it via a plain
 * `phys_to_virt()`, which needs this region to stay part of the kernel's
 * ordinary linear map, not excluded from it.
 */
&{/reserved-memory} {
	sec_log_mem: sec_log_region@9f200000 {
		reg = <0x0 0x9f200000 0x0 0x200000>;
	};
};

/*
 * Round 34 (2026-09-19, docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md): the S9 Ultra
 * mainline porting project hit our exact symptom class (Linux boots,
 * console/logo visible, then dies with zero pstore/panic evidence) and
 * traced an early instance of it to `TZBSP_ERR_FATAL_NOC_ERROR` - Linux
 * touching a physical range Samsung's stock DTBO normally reserves but
 * their minimal upstream DTB didn't carry. Our own DTS has the same gap:
 * pulled this device's *live* `/reserved-memory` node straight off real
 * hardware (`adb shell` against a TWRP boot, which uses the same
 * ABL-merged stock DTB/DTBO as normal boot) and diffed it against what
 * upstream's `sm8250.dtsi` + `sm8250-samsung-common.dtsi` already
 * declare (`work/round34-reserved-mem/live-reserved-memory.txt`).
 *
 * Everything below is either populated by ABL/XBL/TrustZone before any
 * kernel choice is even made (so it's live/protected the same way
 * whether Samsung's downstream kernel or our own mainline one is
 * running), or is a case where upstream's own static node undershoots
 * what this specific device's firmware actually protects. The `pil_*`
 * remoteproc firmware regions were also compared and found shifted vs.
 * upstream, but were deliberately left alone: those addresses are only
 * ever touched by an active PIL/remoteproc driver requesting them, none
 * of which this Phase 1 kernel enables, and we have direct proof
 * (`docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md` Round 30+) that code executing from
 * uniLoader's own `PAYLOAD_ENTRY` - which happens to sit inside the
 * live `cdsp_secure_heap` range - runs correctly for many seconds, so
 * that particular overlap isn't hardware-enforced against us. These are:
 *
 *  - sec_debug_region@0x9f000000 (8 MiB) actually encloses our own
 *    sec_log_region above; reserved here as the two flanking pieces so
 *    the two nodes don't overlap.
 *  - ss_plog_region, hdm_region, kaslr_region: small, unconditional
 *    Samsung/TZ carveouts right next to sec_log/ramoops, entirely
 *    unreserved in our DTB until now.
 *  - dfps_data_region@0x9e300000 sits immediately after
 *    cont_splash_mem/our simple-framebuffer's own carveout ends -
 *    exactly the kind of neighbor worth reserving given our current
 *    unsolved "screen goes black right after simplefb registers"
 *    symptom.
 *  - tima_region/rkp_region/uh_heap_region: contiguous
 *    0xb0100000-0xb1800000, Samsung's kernel-protection carveouts.
 *  - sec_debug_rdx_bootdev@0x100000000: a large (173 MiB) live
 *    reservation with no upstream equivalent at all.
 */
&{/reserved-memory} {
	sec_debug_region_lo@9f000000 {
		reg = <0x0 0x9f000000 0x0 0x200000>;
		no-map;
	};

	sec_debug_region_hi@9f400000 {
		reg = <0x0 0x9f400000 0x0 0x400000>;
		no-map;
	};

	ss_plog_region@9f800000 {
		reg = <0x0 0x9f800000 0x0 0x200000>;
		no-map;
	};

	hdm_region@9fb00000 {
		reg = <0x0 0x9fb00000 0x0 0x1000>;
		no-map;
	};

	kaslr_region@a0001000 {
		reg = <0x0 0xa0001000 0x0 0x1000>;
		no-map;
	};

	dfps_data_region@9e300000 {
		reg = <0x0 0x9e300000 0x0 0x100000>;
		no-map;
	};

	tima_region@b0100000 {
		reg = <0x0 0xb0100000 0x0 0x100000>;
		no-map;
	};

	rkp_region@b0200000 {
		reg = <0x0 0xb0200000 0x0 0x200000>;
		no-map;
	};

	uh_heap_region@b0400000 {
		reg = <0x0 0xb0400000 0x0 0x1400000>;
		no-map;
	};

	sec_debug_rdx_bootdev@100000000 {
		reg = <0x1 0x00000000 0x0 0xad00000>;
		no-map;
	};
};

/*
 * Round 34: upstream's static `xbl_aop_mem`/`removed_mem` nodes both
 * undershoot what this device's live firmware actually protects -
 * confirmed via the same live `/reserved-memory` pull above. Real live
 * xbl_aop starts a full ~0xfd000 (~1 MiB) earlier than upstream's
 * `0x80700000`, and removed_mem is 0x800000 (8 MiB) bigger than
 * upstream's `0x5300000`. Overriding both by their upstream labels to
 * match the live values exactly, rather than adding overlapping nodes.
 */
&xbl_aop_mem {
	reg = <0x0 0x80603000 0x0 0x25d000>;
};

&removed_mem {
	reg = <0x0 0x80b00000 0x0 0x5b00000>;
};

/*
 * Round 23 (2026-09-19, docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md): pulled Samsung's
 * persistent `/dev/block/by-name/debug` ramdump-summary partition (a
 * genuine, real crash-history log spanning many prior resets - unlike
 * `/proc/last_kmsg`'s small ring buffer, found via online research the
 * owner asked for after confirming no UART hardware was available)
 * directly off the physical device from TWRP. It captured a real Linux
 * kernel panic from one of the Round 20-22 uniLoader boot attempts, with
 * a full backtrace pointing at `out_of_memory` / `__alloc_pages_nodemask`
 * / `handle_pte_fault` / `do_page_fault` - a real, textbook Linux
 * page-fault-triggers-allocation-triggers-OOM call chain, panic message
 * "System is deadlocked on memory". Root cause found directly:
 * upstream's `sm8250.dtsi` root `memory@80000000` node ships with
 * `reg = <0x0 0x80000000 0x0 0x0>` (zero size) and an explicit comment,
 * "We expect the bootloader to fill in the size" - normally patched
 * dynamically by ABL at boot time, but uniLoader's `patch_dtb()` only
 * ever adds `linux,initrd-start`/`-end`, never touches memory size. This
 * project's own compiled DTB confirmed carrying that exact zero-size
 * placeholder through unmodified. The real kernel was booting believing
 * it had zero bytes of usable RAM - a complete, sufficient explanation
 * for the observed OOM panic, independent of anything display/cmdline
 * related (Round 21-22's fixes never had a chance to matter). Real size
 * confirmed directly from this device's own hardware log
 * (`work/last_kmsg_*.txt`, XBL: "Rank 0 size = 6144 MB, Rank 1 size =
 * 0 MB"), base address matches the placeholder's own existing `0x80000000`.
 */
&{/memory@80000000} {
	reg = <0x0 0x80000000 0x1 0x80000000>;
};

/*
 * Round 22 (2026-09-19, docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md): uniLoader confirmed
 * working end to end on real hardware (its own splash/console prints
 * correctly, reaches "Booting kernel..." and jumps) - but nothing visible
 * happens after that, and there's no way yet to tell whether the real
 * kernel is silently working fine (just genuinely has no configured
 * console) or has crashed. Overriding the inherited phone-resolution
 * `framebuffer` node from `sm8250-samsung-common.dtsi` (1080x2400,
 * upstream's Galaxy S20 default) with this tablet's real panel geometry -
 * the node's `reg`/`format` are already correct (same physical splash
 * region ABL itself draws to, `cont_splash_region@9c000000`, already
 * `no-map`'d in `/reserved-memory` by the same shared base file). Also
 * forcing a real cmdline via `chosen/bootargs` here (uniLoader's own
 * `patch_dtb()` only adds `linux,initrd-start`/`-end`, never touches
 * `bootargs`) - `CONFIG_CMDLINE_FORCE` in `kernel/config/gts7l.fragment`
 * is a second, independent way to guarantee this reaches the kernel
 * regardless of what devicetree merging does.
 */
&framebuffer {
	width = <1600>;
	height = <2560>;
	stride = <(1600 * 4)>;
};

&{/chosen} {
	bootargs = "console=tty0 loglevel=15";
};

/*
 * Volume Up is wired to PM8150L (the companion/"L" PMIC), not PM8150
 * (primary) as on the common.dtsi's Galaxy S20 phone reference. Confirmed
 * via the GPL overlay's DT symbol table entry:
 *   key_vol_up_default = ".../qcom,pm8150l@4/pinctrl@c000/key_vol_up/..."
 * i.e. pin "gpio3" on pm8150l, not pm8150. Also cross-confirmed live:
 * `/proc/interrupts` shows `volume_up` on the `pmic_arb` domain, and
 * `/sys/kernel/debug/gpio`'s pm8150l gpiochip (gpiochip2, GPIOs 1085-1093)
 * has gpio3 configured as an input with a pull-up - consistent with a
 * button line, not a phone's pm8150 gpio3 wiring.
 *
 * This overrides (not extends) the inherited common.dtsi gpio_keys node,
 * which points at &vol_up_n on the wrong PMIC for this board.
 */
/delete-node/ &gpio_keys;

/ {
	gpio_keys: gpio-keys {
		compatible = "gpio-keys";

		pinctrl-names = "default";
		pinctrl-0 = <&vol_up_n_gts7l>;

		key-vol-up {
			label = "Volume Up";
			gpios = <&pm8150l_gpios 3 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
			linux,code = <KEY_VOLUMEUP>;
			debounce-interval = <15>;
			linux,can-disable;
			wakeup-source;
		};
	};
};

&pm8150l_gpios {
	vol_up_n_gts7l: vol-up-n-gts7l-state {
		pins = "gpio3";
		function = "normal";
		power-source = <0>;
		input-enable;
		bias-pull-up;
	};
};

/*
 * Volume Down and Power both ride the PM8150 PON block's resin/pwrkey
 * lines, already wired up correctly by sm8250-samsung-common.dtsi
 * (&pon_pwrkey, &pon_resin -> KEY_VOLUMEDOWN) - confirmed live via
 * `/proc/interrupts` showing `pon_kpdpwr_status` and `pon_resin_status` on
 * the pmic_arb domain. No override needed here.
 */

/*
 * Touchscreen: Novatek NT36523 TDDI (same IC drives the display panel -
 * see docs/hardware-inventory.md). Wired in Samsung's DT as "novatek,nvt-ts",
 * not yet backed by a mainline driver/binding - this node records the real
 * wiring now so the binding can be filled in during Phase 2 without
 * re-deriving it.
 *
 * irq-gpio = tlmm 15, confirmed two independent ways:
 *   - GPL overlay: `novatek,irq-gpio = <0xffffffff 0xf 0x2002>` (0xf = 15)
 *   - Live: `/proc/interrupts` shows `nvt-ts` on `msmgpio 15`, Edge
 * TODO(phase2): I2C bus/address (reg = <0x62> per the GPL source, bus
 * segment not yet identified), reset-gpio, and VDD/VCC-I2C supply rails are
 * not yet confirmed - the GPL node only specifies the irq line and firmware
 * blob name (novatek,firmware_name = "tsp_novatek/nt36523_gts7l.bin").
 */
&tlmm {
	nvt_ts_int_gts7l: nvt-ts-int-state {
		pins = "gpio15";
		function = "gpio";
		bias-disable;
	};
};

/*
 * S Pen: Wacom W90xx-series EMR digitizer over I2C (reg = <0x56> per the
 * GPL source). No BLE - this is a plain passive digitizer, unlike the S9
 * Ultra project's BLE-capable S Pen.
 *
 * All three GPIOs below are confirmed two independent ways:
 *   - GPL overlay: irq-gpio=0x88(136), pdct-gpio=0x7(7), fwe-gpio=0x6(6)
 *   - Live: `/proc/interrupts` shows `sec_epen_irq` on `msmgpio 136`
 *     (Level) and `sec_epen_pdct` on `msmgpio 7` (Edge)
 * AVDD supply confirmed from a second instance of this node in the GPL
 * source tree: `wacom,regulator_avdd = "pm8150_l13"` - i.e. PM8150 LDO13.
 * Not yet mapped to a mainline regulator phandle/supply property.
 *
 * TODO(phase4, tracks roadmap Phase 4): actual mainline driver binding
 * (upstream has no "wacom,w90xx" binding yet - closest is the generic
 * Wacom I2C digitizer support, needs evaluation), IC type 0x233d and
 * boot_addr 0x9 (bootloader-mode alternate address) recorded above for
 * whoever writes that driver glue.
 */
&tlmm {
	epen_int_gts7l: epen-int-state {
		pins = "gpio136";
		function = "gpio";
		bias-disable;
	};

	epen_pdct_gts7l: epen-pdct-state {
		pins = "gpio7";
		function = "gpio";
		bias-disable;
	};

	epen_fwe_gts7l: epen-fwe-state {
		pins = "gpio6";
		function = "gpio";
		bias-disable;
	};
};

/*
 * Charger + fuel gauge + MUIC + haptics: Maxim MAX77705 (reg = <0x66> per
 * the GPL source). irq-gpio confirmed two independent ways:
 *   - GPL overlay: `max77705,irq-gpio = <0x92 0xb 0x1>` - phandle 0x92
 *     resolves (via the same overlay's own symbol table) to the PM8150L
 *     gpio-controller node, pin 11 ("gpio11"), active-low.
 *   - Live: `/sys/kernel/debug/gpio`'s pm8150l gpiochip (gpiochip2) shows
 *     gpio11 configured as an input with a pull-up, consistent with an
 *     active-low interrupt line. `/proc/interrupts` additionally shows a
 *     large family of `max77705` interrupt names (chgin-irq, fuelgauge-irq,
 *     muic-*, pd-*, usbc-* etc.) all live and firing on the running stock
 *     kernel, confirming this is the real, active charger/PD/MUIC chip.
 *
 * TODO(phase3): mainline has no "maxim,max77705" driver - this is a
 * Samsung/Maxim combo IC (charger + fuel gauge + MUIC + haptic + USB-PD)
 * that will need real driver work, not just a devicetree node. VDD supply
 * rail not yet identified.
 */
&pm8150l_gpios {
	max77705_int_gts7l: max77705-int-state {
		pins = "gpio11";
		function = "normal";
		power-source = <0>;
		input-enable;
		bias-pull-up;
	};
};

/*
 * Wi-Fi/BT combo: Qualcomm QCA6390 over PCIe (confirmed live -
 * `wlan_wake_irq` and the WLAN_CE_* MSI vectors sit on the `msm_pci_msi`
 * domain in /proc/interrupts, meaning this is the PCIe-attached ath11k
 * path, not SDIO). CONFIG_CNSS_QCA6390=y confirmed in Samsung's own
 * defconfig (docs/hardware-inventory.md).
 *
 * TODO(phase3): the actual PCIe root complex / wlan node instantiation
 * (&pcie1 or equivalent on this platform, enable/reset GPIOs, regulator
 * supplies) has not been identified yet - needs its own pass through the
 * GPL source's PCIe and regulator nodes, which are large and not yet
 * cross-referenced against a live confirmation the way the items above
 * were. Tracked as a Phase 3 task, not attempted in this first-pass DTS.
 */

/*
 * NOT YET ADDRESSED IN THIS FILE (left for their respective phases rather
 * than guessed at):
 *   - Display panel (Novatek NT36523 / PPA957DB1 WQXGA LCD) - Phase 2
 *   - Adreno 650 GPU enablement - Phase 2
 *   - microSD, USB host - Phase 4
 *   - Book Cover Keyboard (stm,touchpad / stm,keypad pogo-pin nodes) - Phase 5
 * See plans/roadmap.md for the phase breakdown these map to.
 */

&adsp {
	firmware-name = "qcom/sm8250/Samsung/gts7l/adsp.mbn";
	status = "okay";
};

&cdsp {
	firmware-name = "qcom/sm8250/Samsung/gts7l/cdsp.mbn";
	status = "okay";
};

&slpi {
	firmware-name = "qcom/sm8250/Samsung/gts7l/slpi.mbn";
	status = "okay";
};

/*
 * Round 17/18 (2026-09-17, docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md): after getting
 * ABL to accept this project's own mainline DTB cleanly (the S9 Ultra
 * project's invalidated-dtbo/DeviceTreeAppended technique), the device
 * started auto-resetting (`PM: HARD RESET by PS_HOLD`) immediately after
 * kernel handoff - closely paralleling a TrustZone NoC fault the S9 Ultra
 * project hit at this exact stage, caused by their mainline DTS missing
 * `/reserved-memory` carveouts their device's real firmware actually
 * protects.
 *
 * Audited this project's own compiled DTB against stock's real
 * `/reserved-memory` node (extracted directly from stock's boot.img `dtb`
 * section) address-by-address, not just by presence/absence. Found one
 * concrete, high-confidence mismatch: upstream's `sm8250.dtsi` reserves
 * `xbl_aop_mem` (Always-On Processor firmware memory - queried very early
 * in boot, during RPMh/power-domain setup) at `0x80700000`, size
 * `0x160000`. Stock's real firmware reserves `0x80603000`, size
 * `0x25d000` - same end address (`0x80860000`), but starting ~1MiB
 * *earlier*. That ~1MiB gap (`0x80603000`-`0x80700000`) is real,
 * secure-world-owned AOP firmware memory on this actual hardware that
 * upstream's generic reservation doesn't cover at all - our kernel's
 * buddy allocator could legitimately hand that range out to any driver,
 * which is exactly the kind of access that trips a NoC/TrustZone security
 * fault. This is the leading suspect for the Round 17 auto-reset.
 *
 * Also found `dfps_data_region@9e300000` (size 0x100000, dynamic
 * frame-rate tracking data) present in stock's reserved-memory list with
 * no upstream equivalent at all - added here too, matching stock's own
 * `no-map`-less (normal cached-accessible carveout, not excluded from the
 * linear map) treatment rather than guessing.
 *
 * Not yet re-tested on hardware as of writing this comment - see
 * kernel-boot-debugging.md for whichever Round actually confirms or
 * refutes this fixes the auto-reset.
 */
&xbl_aop_mem {
	reg = <0x0 0x80603000 0x0 0x25d000>;
};

&{/reserved-memory} {
	dfps_data_mem: dfps_data_region@9e300000 {
		reg = <0x0 0x9e300000 0x0 0x100000>;
	};
};

/*
 * Round 35 (2026-09-19, docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md): the real,
 * mechanistically-confirmed cause of "screen goes black immediately
 * after `fb0: simplefb registered!`" (Round 33's `clk_ignore_unused` and
 * Round 34's reserved-memory audit both left this untouched, because
 * this isn't a devicetree-completeness problem at all).
 *
 * `CONFIG_SM_DISPCC_8250=y` is a *built-in* driver (no module loading
 * needed, unlike `CONFIG_DRM_MSM=m` which never gets loaded by this
 * minimal initramfs) - so it auto-probes unconditionally during normal
 * boot. Read `drivers/clk/qcom/dispcc-sm8250.c`'s own
 * `disp_cc_sm8250_probe()` directly: for our exact compatible string
 * ("qcom,sm8250-dispcc", not the sm8150/sm8350 variants handled
 * elsewhere in that function), it unconditionally calls
 * `clk_lucid_pll_configure(&disp_cc_pll0, ...)` and the same for pll1 -
 * reprogramming both display PLLs from scratch on every single probe,
 * with no check for "is this already locked and actively clocking
 * something". That's normal and expected on mainline *if* a real
 * MDSS/DPU/panel driver runs right after to redo the whole
 * power-sequenced re-init (GDSC, reset, PLL relock, panel commands) -
 * but we don't have one (`CONFIG_DRM_MSM=m`, never loaded here), so
 * dispcc tears down whatever PLL state ABL left actively driving this
 * LCD's video-mode DSI link, and nothing ever restores it. Unlike a
 * command-mode/self-refreshing AMOLED panel (which just keeps showing
 * its last frame from internal GRAM), a video-mode LCD panel - which is
 * genuinely what this tablet has, confirmed by the owner correcting an
 * earlier assumption - loses sync and blanks the instant the DSI host
 * stops actively streaming, which is exactly the "immediately after
 * simplefb registers" timing that's been observed since Round 32.
 *
 * Confirmed every consumer of `&dispcc`'s clocks/power-domain/resets in
 * `sm8250.dtsi` is an MDSS/MDP/DSI/DP child node, all only ever bound by
 * the same never-loaded `CONFIG_DRM_MSM` module - nothing built into
 * this kernel actually needs dispcc's clocks at boot. Disabling the node
 * entirely keeps its driver from probing (and thus from touching the
 * PLLs) for the rest of Phase 1; this is explicitly a stopgap for the
 * "leave ABL's splash content on screen" approach, not a real display
 * fix - actually driving the panel from Linux (Phase 2 scope) will need
 * dispcc probing properly alongside a real MDSS/DPU/panel driver that
 * redoes the full re-init sequence, not avoiding it.
 */
&dispcc {
	status = "disabled";
};
